POTS Secondary to hEDS
Complex Fatigue Entities
Connective tissue laxity driving vascular pooling

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: CFE-039
Reason for Visit: Urgent evaluation of severe tachycardia, recurrent pre-syncope, and diffuse joint pain preventing basic mobility.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported an acute inability to stand without blacking out, accompanied by severe generalized joint pain and heart palpitations. Immediate physical assessment was required to rule out acute structural joint dislocation and primary cardiac arrhythmia, and to establish hemodynamic safety prior to standard clinic hours.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: Every joint in my body is aching, my heart races wildly if I sit up, and the exhaustion is completely paralyzing.
History of Present Illness: Patient with known hypermobile Ehlers-Danlos Syndrome presents with a severe acute flare of secondary dysautonomia. Over the past 24 hours, the patient reports severe joint hypermobility leading to diffuse myofascial pain. Concurrently, the connective tissue laxity is driving profound vascular pooling, resulting in severe orthostatic tachycardia, dizziness, and chronic fatigue. Patient states they cannot walk to the bathroom without their vision graying out completely.
Functional Status: Bedbound; crawling or sliding on the floor to access the toilet to avoid syncope.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; extremely high fall risk due to combined joint instability and orthostatic hypotension.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 115/75 supine, 95/65 standing mmHg | HR: 80 supine, 138 standing bpm | Temp: 36.7 C | RR: 16 /min | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 70 Kg
General: Exhausted, moving carefully to protect joints, pale upon sitting upright.
Physical Exam: Postural vital signs demonstrate profound tachycardia and borderline hypotension upon standing. Beighton score is 7/9, confirming marked joint hypermobility. Shoulders and knees are hypermobile but structurally located; no gross dislocations. Diffuse myofascial tenderness present globally. Cardiovascular exam is otherwise unremarkable. The clinical picture clearly demonstrates connective tissue laxity driving vascular pooling.
Environmental Observations: Clear pathways; patient has intentionally moved mattress to the floor to prevent falls.
Medication Reconciliation: No new medications. Uses paracetamol for joint pain with minimal effect.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: POTS Secondary to hEDS - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Occult Bleeding (e.g., GI bleed) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Normal supine blood pressure, absence of melena or hematemesis, and a chronic history of identical structurally driven flares.

Cardiogenic Syncope - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Tachycardia is perfectly correlated with positional changes and rapidly normalizes when supine.

Acute Joint Dislocation - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Normal anatomical contours, preservation of range of motion (though painful), and intact peripheral neurovascular status.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The simultaneous presentation of severe dysautonomia and widespread joint pain mandated an urgent, in-home physical evaluation to definitively exclude life-threatening cardiac arrhythmias, occult bleeding causing hypovolemia, and acute orthopedic emergencies. Having established that the crisis is functional—driven by inherent connective tissue laxity allowing excessive vascular pooling—immediate, non-pharmacological hemodynamic stabilization was required. Transferring this highly unstable, hypermobile patient to an ED would have caused immense physical trauma and worsened the dysautonomic crash.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed high-volume oral rehydration directly on-site and instituted strict horizontal resting orders. Targeted intervention strategy includes Vitamin C, LDN, and Magnesium. Advised concurrent use of joint bracing to support hypermobile limbs during essential transfers.
Positive Behavioral Change: By systematically wearing a tight abdominal binder before attempting to stand, you will mechanically compress your highly elastic abdominal veins, directly forcing pooled blood back to your heart and rewarding yourself with a stable heart rate and clear vision.
Home-Based Services Optional: Referral to physiotherapy specializing in hypermobility.
DME/Safety Optional: Abdominal binder and joint support braces recommended.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 1 week to optimize dysautonomia medications (e.g., Ivabradine, Midodrine) and physical therapy.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation via 000 if patient actually faints and hits the head, if a joint pops out and remains physically deformed/stuck, or if breathing becomes labored.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Because your blood vessels are built with extra-stretchy collagen, standard POTS treatments require even more aggressive mechanical compression to be effective.
Clinical Course: The orthostatic symptoms will slowly improve over 48 hours with aggressive salt, fluid, and compression therapy. Joint pain requires careful pacing and stabilization.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Call 000 immediately if you actually pass out, or if your shoulder or knee pops out and you cannot get it back into place.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The stretchy connective tissue in your body means your veins act like balloons, letting blood pool in your legs and stomach when you stand; the salt water and abdominal binder physically push that blood back up to your brain.
Actionable Steps: Perform all grooming and dressing activities while seated tomorrow; avoiding prolonged standing entirely prevents vascular pooling, positively reinforcing a steady baseline of energy and preventing dizzy spells.

